This hook aggregates eight unrelated Onyx subscriptions (`iouReport`, `policy`, `ownerLogin`, `userBillingGracePeriodEnds`, `iouReportNextStep`, `amountOwed`, `ownerBillingGracePeriodEnd`, `delegateEmail`) into a single grab-bag return object. These values serve different concerns (report data, policy, owner identity, billing grace periods, delegate email) and change independently, so every consumer re-renders whenever *any* of them changes. The consumers also only use a subset each — `PayActionButton` destructures 6 of 8, `ApproveActionButton` reads only `iouReport` directly — which is exactly the over-subscription this rule guards against.

Split the subscriptions into cohesive hooks grouped by concern (e.g. a report/policy hook vs. a billing-grace hook vs. an owner/delegate identity hook), so each consumer subscribes only to the data it actually uses and re-renders stay narrow:

```ts
function useReportPreviewReportAndPolicy(iouReportID: string | undefined) {
    const [iouReport] = useOnyx(`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT}${iouReportID}`);
    const [policy] = useOnyx(`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.POLICY}${iouReport?.policyID}`);
    return {iouReport, policy};
}

function useReportPreviewBillingGrace(iouReportID: string | undefined) {
    const [userBillingGracePeriodEnds] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.SHARED_NVP_PRIVATE_USER_BILLING_GRACE_PERIOD_END);
    const [amountOwed] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.NVP_PRIVATE_AMOUNT_OWED);
    const [ownerBillingGracePeriodEnd] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.NVP_PRIVATE_OWNER_BILLING_GRACE_PERIOD_END);
    const [iouReportNextStep] = useOnyx(`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.NEXT_STEP}${iouReportID}`);
    return {userBillingGracePeriodEnds, amountOwed, ownerBillingGracePeriodEnd, iouReportNextStep};
}
```

`getCurrencyDecimals` captures `currencyList` directly in its closure (line 24) and lists it as a dependency (`[currencyList]`), so it gets a new identity every time the currency list changes. This causes `actionsValue` to update on every `currencyList` change, which re-renders all consumers of `useCurrencyListActions()` — defeating the purpose of the state/actions split.

`getCurrencySymbol` on line 18 already solves this correctly by reading from `currencyListRef.current` with an empty dependency array.

Apply the same ref pattern to `getCurrencyDecimals`:

```ts
const getCurrencyDecimals = useCallback(
    (currencyCode: string | undefined): number => {
        const decimals = currencyListRef.current[currencyCode ?? ""]?.decimals;
        return decimals ?? 2;
    },
    [],
);
```

